WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.196 --> 00:00:10.863
(organ playing)

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:25.153
- Good morning.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.800
Class of 2023, transfer
students, Eli Whitney students,

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.750
and visiting international
students, welcome to Yale.

00:00:36.631 --> 00:00:39.798
(audience applauding)

00:00:46.694 --> 00:00:49.380
I am Marvin Chun, Dean of Yale College,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.300
and I'm so pleased to be with you

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.560
here on this beautiful morning.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:55.160
Joining me onstage to greet you

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.063
are President Peter
Salovey, Provost Ben Polak,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.700
Secretary Kim Goff-Crews,

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.610
Faculty of Arts and
Sciences Dean Tamar Gendler,

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.760
Graduate School Dean Lynn Cooley,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.030
Athletic Director Vicky Chun,

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.610
the Yale Alumni Association's

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.950
Executive Director Weili Cheng,

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:14.410
officers of the university,

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.900
heads and deans of residential colleges,

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.973
and deans and members of the
Yale College Dean's Office.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.220
Family members and friends
who are here today,

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:26.470
we extend our warm welcome to you

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.050
and we thank you for
everything that you have done

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.713
to support and guide these young adults.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.330
In my role as Dean, one of my jobs

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.170
is to support President Salovey's mission

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.300
for Yale to be the research university

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.300
most committed to teaching and learning.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.790
What that means for you students

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.610
is that I am here to make sure

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.500
that you get the best of
what Yale has to offer,

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.823
in the classroom or outside of it.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.180
A good part of what you will learn here

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:56.630
will come from the faculty,

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.020
who will teach you in the
classes you will take,

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.530
while much will come from the people

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.403
sitting here with you today,
your peers and colleagues.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.760
Taken together, these teachers of yours

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.230
will give you the liberal education

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:09.723
that is Yale's hallmark.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.350
By the time you graduate,

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.120
you will have furnished your mind

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.220
in the broadest possible sense,

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.340
not by pursuing narrow, specialized study,

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.510
but instead by searching
for new knowledge,

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.510
new perspectives, new ways
of looking at the world,

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.063
and by adding your own.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.450
If you make good use of this place,

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:31.460
you will discover fields of knowledge

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.200
that are completely new to you,

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.150
that you haven't even heard of,

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:38.730
and you will open yourselves
to them and learn from them.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.160
And when you get to know your colleagues

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.470
sitting around here today,

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.290
something else you will discover

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:47.560
is that you come from every
possible walk of life,

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.320
bringing with you every imaginable talent,

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.023
every experience, every world view.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:55.313
Learn from them too.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.460
As a community, you are politically

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.023
liberal, conservative, or moderate.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.000
You are people with and
without disabilities.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.573
You observe different religions, or none.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.320
Roughly two out of three of you

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.053
attended public high schools.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.190
About one out of five of you

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.463
are eligible for Federal Pell grants.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.100
Over one out of six of you

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.453
are first-generation college students.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:23.900
About one out of nine of you

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.923
are international students
from over 50 countries.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.983
More than half of you
identify as students of color.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.733
And you are of all
genders and sexualities.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.110
We should take none of this for granted.

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:42.630
Think for a minute that
for most of Yale's history,

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.240
as recently as 50 years ago,

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.980
even when U.S. was able to
send humans to the moon,

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.090
women were not allowed

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.513
to receive an education in Yale College.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.200
I think of my mother, who
along with my late father

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.893
immigrated to California from South Korea.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.620
Now one of the world's
most prosperous countries,

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:07.030
and here's why I get to ask,

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:09.300
how many of you know BTS?

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.690
Okay, good, I think we're good, see.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:14.035
No hands raised behind me, right?

00:04:14.035 --> 00:04:16.000
(audience laughing)

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.000
South Korea was one of the
poorest countries back then.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.560
But even in the 1950s,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.860
right after a devastating war,

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.810
my mom was able to receive
a college education

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.523
from Korea's version of Yale.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.210
And without the opportunities
afforded to her,

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.260
I know that I would not be standing here

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:38.123
as a faculty member of this great place.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.680
You are coming to Yale as it
marks a historic milestone,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.400
the anniversary of 50 years of
coeducation in Yale College,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.400
and 150 years of coeducation

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.763
in the graduate and professional schools.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.500
This year the university honors

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.330
the monumental value that coeducation

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.440
has brought to our institution,

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:01.410
not only because coeducation was such a

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.760
huge step forward in creating
today's Yale community,

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.070
but also because it went to

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:09.010
the heart of a Yale liberal education,

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:13.150
open to all people as
well as to all ideas.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.700
You will hear the word excellent

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.890
and diversity together
here, again and again.

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:20.400
At Yale they go hand-in-hand.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.140
If you look at Yale's history,

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.870
as its excellence has continued

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:26.860
and grown every decade, every year,

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.683
so has the diversity of its
students, staff, and faculty.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.840
The connection between
excellence and diversity

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.550
has long captured the
attention of researchers

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.260
who study collective intelligence,

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:42.590
and who investigate whether groups

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:44.863
are smarter than the individuals in them.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.710
Carnegie Mellon Management
Professor Anita Woolley

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.920
studies teams and what makes them stronger

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.120
found that it is not
the group's average IQ,

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.423
or even the IQ of its smartest member,

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.660
and it isn't the number,
it is not the number

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.713
of extroverted members or
the group's motivation.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.100
What she found, and here
she is in her own words,

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:12.420
was that the individual
skills most critical

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:15.320
for collective intelligence are those

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:18.010
that enhance the ability of group members

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:21.320
to collaborate effectively, or the skills

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:23.670
that enrich the collaboration by bringing

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:26.293
a sufficient diversity of perspectives.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.730
These are the conditions
of collective intelligence,

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:33.730
and what they do is they predict a group's

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:38.140
superior performance on a
wide range of complex tasks.

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:39.600
This could mean playing checkers,

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.520
brainstorming possible uses of a brick,

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.660
optimizing choices in
behavioral economic games,

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.703
or even competing in League of Legends.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.450
Yale embraces the idea of
collective intelligence,

00:06:52.450 --> 00:06:55.340
which depends on people
who bring diverse ways

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.240
of looking at the world, and who know

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.493
how to communicate and collaborate.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:02.560
And here you are.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.690
You have earned a place in this community,

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:07.880
which is itself a team,
because of the promise

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.413
you have shown in what
you will contribute to it,

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.850
and to the communities and teams

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:15.670
you will join after you graduate.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.910
And as you think about how to do that,

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:20.490
you can start by asking how to maximize

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.400
your collective intelligence,

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.820
by leveraging your different backgrounds,

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:28.070
views, talents, and interests,

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:30.730
and how you can enhance
your social sensitivity

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:32.580
as a community, by learning

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.543
how to see the world
through each other's eyes.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:40.140
As a psychology professor
let me demonstrate

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:42.980
one way you might start out on this road,

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.700
and so here please pull
out your printed program

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:47.773
and inside you'll find an insert.

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:52.110
Take it out, and you'll see the insert

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:54.323
with the big image on it, okay?

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:59.673
So look at the image, the
first image on the top,

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:06.440
and then name out-loud,
when I say go, what you see.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.150
Okay, go.

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:09.280
(audience speaking aloud)

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:10.680
Good, thank you.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.360
If you first saw a duck, raise your hand.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:15.770
Okay and if you first saw
a rabbit, raise your hand.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:20.703
Okay, half-half are pretty
evenly split, alright.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:23.610
And then now you can
try seeing it both ways.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:24.620
If you saw it as a duck,

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:27.613
try to see it as a rabbit, and vice versa.

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:33.063
If you have trouble seeing it both ways,

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.770
anyone having trouble seeing it both ways?

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:39.620
The admissions director is behind me.

00:08:43.961 --> 00:08:45.610
So this simple demonstration

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:49.060
shows a fundamental
principle of psychology,

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.310
that we all experience
the world differently.

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:55.650
Our perceptual interpretations,
as in the case you just saw,

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.700
but also our memories, our thoughts,

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:00.830
our attitudes, are all
constructs of the mind,

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:02.720
and they differ across people,

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:06.673
even when considering the same
object, event, or evidence.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.950
Yale will expose you to views
different from your own,

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:15.230
and when it does, you can
start by finding common ground

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:18.190
with people when you
don't agree with them.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.350
So before you put away your insert,

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:24.003
take one more look at it, this
time on the bottom carton,

00:09:24.990 --> 00:09:28.943
and you can see that there are
two armies facing each other.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.133
Think of U.S.-China trade relations.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:36.340
And then look at the army on the bottom,

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.510
and at the front of
that army on the bottom

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:42.380
there's a general who's
looking back at his troops,

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:46.064
and he's urging them
with the following comic,

00:09:46.064 --> 00:09:48.043
with the following caption.

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:52.380
There can be no peace until they renounce

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:55.373
their rabbit god and accept our duck god.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:09:59.320
Okay so imagine what happens when

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:03.020
the opposing troops realize
they are serving the same flag.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.513
And so you can ask
yourself, what is my flag?

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:08.630
So here's our final exercise.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:10.820
Please shout out the
answer to the following,

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:12.770
your first quiz at Yale.

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.280
What's the research
university most committed

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:17.768
to teaching and learning,
please shout it out.

00:10:17.768 --> 00:10:18.930
- [Audience] Yale!

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.323
- Good, you are all now
members of Bulldog Nation,

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.860
with the sophomores, juniors, and seniors,

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:27.810
and alumni, faculty, and staff,

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:31.040
and with each other,
you have common ground,

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:34.470
an immediate shared bond
that is more than tribal.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.320
It goes deeper to the
intellectual curiosity

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.220
and the commitment to community

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:40.893
that have brought you all here.

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:44.600
You have arrived at a place
that values difference

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:46.630
and actively seeks it out.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:50.480
It is how professors and
students find and test knowledge,

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:53.540
and it is how the university
equips its students

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:56.043
to become leaders and
citizens of the world.

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:58.920
But here the value of seeking difference

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.600
is even more than that.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.590
It is taken as a article of faith,

00:11:02.590 --> 00:11:03.960
it is the coin of the realm,

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:06.040
it is one of the bedrock principles

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:08.623
that governs us in and
out of the classroom.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:11.320
And so let me assume that you came here

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:12.990
not only to find knowledge,

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:15.590
but to live lives of consequence,

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:18.230
and if that's true, you have
come to the right place.

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.480
And as you take your first steps, then,

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:23.220
you will need to become
comfortable with difference,

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:26.873
not just to accept it or
tolerate it, but to insist on it.

00:11:28.334 --> 00:11:29.590
You will find guides everywhere,

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:30.830
in the classes you will take,

00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:32.150
the activities you will join,

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.950
and most of all, again, from
the people sitting around you

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:37.760
right now, your peers and classmates.

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:38.870
All of them are part of

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:40.803
the liberal education that awaits you.

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:44.180
Your life at Yale has started,

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:46.770
and in just a few days you
will enrolling in classes,

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.750
joining organizations, or
training with your teams.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:53.230
And as you do that, here's
what I hope you will do.

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:56.230
If you are a duck person,
go find those rabbit people,

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:58.340
and find common ground with them.

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.770
If you are studying all duck courses,

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:02.520
go find the rabbit courses,

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.690
and see what they can teach you.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:07.720
And when you find yourself
rallying around a duck flag,

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:11.320
take a good hard look at the
rabbit flag across the field,

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:13.360
and the people rallying around it,

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:15.993
and remember that you are all Yalies.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:20.410
Members of the Class of
2023, transfer students,

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:23.700
Eli Whitney students, visiting
international students,

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:27.823
you all belong to Yale and
Yale belongs to you, welcome.

00:12:28.943 --> 00:12:32.110
(audience applauding)

00:12:51.517 --> 00:12:54.184
(organ playing)

00:13:40.794 --> 00:13:43.711
(audience singing)

00:14:38.608 --> 00:14:42.775
(audience singing to organ music)

00:16:33.943 --> 00:16:34.923
- Good morning.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:42.500
To all Eli Whitney
students, transfer students,

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:44.170
visiting international students,

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:48.783
and first-year Yale College
students, welcome to Yale.

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:53.077
(audience applauding)

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:02.750
On behalf of my colleagues here onstage,

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:06.510
I extend a warm greeting
to the families here today,

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:09.210
and thank you all for joining us.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.210
Please remember these first moments

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:15.210
of your loved one's college
career are very special,

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:18.243
and I'm glad you can
share them with us today.

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:24.150
Usually in an opening address,

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:27.810
university presidents tell undergraduates

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.670
that they are amazing individuals,

00:17:30.670 --> 00:17:34.170
selected from the among the most talented

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:37.533
high school students in the world today.

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:41.330
This is of course true.

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:43.823
But it is not the point I wanna make.

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:48.590
Instead, I want to encourage you

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:53.590
to approach college unimpressed
by how impressive you are.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:00.083
Have more questions than answers.

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:05.053
Admit to being puzzled or confused.

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:09.070
Be willing to say I don't know,

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:10.303
but I want to find out.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:14.490
And most important, have the courage

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:19.490
to say perhaps I am wrong
and others are right.

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:23.600
This is how you will learn the most

00:18:23.600 --> 00:18:25.963
from your teachers and classmates.

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:31.603
And this is why we have
all come to this place.

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:35.250
We are here to ask questions,

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:37.560
questions about one another,

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:40.103
and about the world around us.

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:46.380
We are here at Yale to nurture
a culture of curiosity.

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:53.640
This summer I read a story
about Isidor Isaac Rabi,

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:57.640
one of the country's most
extraordinary scientists.

00:18:57.640 --> 00:19:02.393
He remembered an important
question his mother asked him.

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:05.820
Brought to this country as an infant,

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:08.540
Rabi conducted research
into particle beams

00:19:08.540 --> 00:19:12.010
that led to the development of the MRI,

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:13.840
and many other scientific advances,

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:17.883
and he won the Nobel
Prize for Physics in 1944.

00:19:19.550 --> 00:19:23.510
Rabi's parents ran a small
grocery store in Brooklyn.

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:26.283
His mother had no formal education.

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:31.110
The other moms, he remembered,
asked their children

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:35.793
every afternoon if they had
learned anything in school.

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:39.750
Not my mother, he recalled.

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:42.343
She always asked me a different question.

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:48.220
Izzie, she would say, did you
ask a good question today?

00:19:50.140 --> 00:19:54.440
He believed her reminder
to ask good questions

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:56.980
helped set him on a path

00:19:56.980 --> 00:19:59.363
to become a distinguished scientist.

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:03.860
So to all the families here today,

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:06.830
when you call your Yale students,

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:10.750
when you ask them about their classes,

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:13.783
and their roommates, and the food,

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:19.780
remember also to ask them
about their questions.

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:25.370
Imagine all the great discoveries

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:28.563
that have come from asking a question.

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:31.330
From Newton's theory of gravity

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:34.940
to the astonishing breakthroughs
in quantum science,

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:37.213
some of which are happening here at Yale.

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:42.430
When a musician experiments
with a new melody,

00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:45.920
or a sociologist observes
a social interaction,

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:48.160
they ask why,

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:50.473
and what would happen if.

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:55.270
Their curiosity lights up our world

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:57.763
and points us in new directions.

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:02.913
Self discoveries come
from asking questions too.

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:06.540
What do you learn when you ask yourself

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:10.133
why did I believe that,
why do I believe that?

00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:14.273
Or why did I do that?

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:19.523
I think of these lines from
the poet Billy Collins:

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:22.670
the trouble with poetry is that

00:21:22.670 --> 00:21:25.493
it encourages the writing of more poetry.

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:30.683
I would say the same of asking questions.

00:21:31.620 --> 00:21:36.603
One leads to another, which
opens doors to still another.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:41.653
Sometimes our questions
lead to a dead-end.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:45.330
We realize the question we asked

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:48.603
wasn't quite right, and a door closes.

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:53.010
But along the way we
have learned something.

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:56.853
Perhaps in the future, we
will ask better questions.

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:03.143
In a well-known scene in
the movie, The Pink Panther,

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:09.160
Inspector Clouseau checks
into a hotel in Germany.

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:13.000
He sees a Dachshund in the lobby,

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:17.737
and he asks the hotel
owner, "Does your dog bite?"

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:20.977
The owner replies, "No."

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:26.983
When Clouseau goes to pet the
dog, it bites his hand, hard.

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:30.967
Shocked, he tells the hotel owner,

00:22:30.967 --> 00:22:34.057
"I thought you said
your dog doesn't bite."

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:39.832
The owner replies, "That is not my dog."

00:22:39.832 --> 00:22:43.360
(audience laughing)

00:22:43.360 --> 00:22:47.863
Inspector Clouseau simply
hadn't asked the right question.

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:53.020
So years ago I taught an
undergraduate seminar,

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:54.900
and one of the questions
on the application

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:59.660
to the course, was, "What
is the most important thing

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:02.127
you've changed your mind about?"

00:23:03.150 --> 00:23:07.490
We were surprised that quite
a few students reported

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:11.183
that they had not changed their
minds about anything at all.

00:23:12.550 --> 00:23:14.420
So we decided to accept to the class

00:23:14.420 --> 00:23:15.740
only students who had changed

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:17.683
their minds about something important.

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:21.093
Be willing to change your mind.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:27.160
Ask questions and embrace
Yale's culture of curiosity.

00:23:27.490 --> 00:23:31.020
Be open to different
viewpoints and experiences.

00:23:31.020 --> 00:23:34.270
See them as opportunities to learn,

00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:36.843
even if sometimes you get your hand bit.

00:23:39.110 --> 00:23:40.670
Now I'm a social psychologist,

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:42.830
and as a graduate student at Yale,

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:46.810
my curiosity was sparked
by the study of emotions,

00:23:46.810 --> 00:23:51.047
and by a question my undergraduate
advisor had asked me.

00:23:51.047 --> 00:23:55.057
"Peter, why do you think
humans even have emotions?

00:23:55.057 --> 00:23:56.737
"What do they do for us?"

00:23:57.850 --> 00:24:01.050
One of my major areas
of research ever since

00:24:01.050 --> 00:24:03.253
has been emotional intelligence.

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:07.630
In our earliest work we
described emotional intelligence

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:10.050
as a set of skills that one can learn,

00:24:10.050 --> 00:24:12.770
that helps you extract the information,

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:16.090
the data, contained in emotions,

00:24:16.090 --> 00:24:18.203
either your own or somebody else's.

00:24:19.440 --> 00:24:20.950
After a few years of research,

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:22.880
it was obvious to me and my collaborators

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:25.923
that we weren't asking
exactly the right question.

00:24:27.150 --> 00:24:31.360
We needed to be able to show
that emotional intelligence

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:36.360
predicted outcomes in life: the
ability to form friendships,

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:40.763
succeed in school, work as
part of a team and the like.

00:24:41.890 --> 00:24:43.830
Trouble was, how do you go about

00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:47.250
measuring the skills of
emotional intelligence?

00:24:47.250 --> 00:24:49.250
We asked ourselves a series of questions,

00:24:49.250 --> 00:24:53.343
starting with, well how are
personal characteristics

00:24:53.343 --> 00:24:56.103
typically measured by psychologists?

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:03.530
The answer is by asking
people to rate themselves.

00:25:03.940 --> 00:25:05.943
These are called self reports.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:10.560
But this led to approaches
that disappointed us.

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.540
How would someone know if
they were the kind of person

00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:16.360
who is especially good
at identifying emotions

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:20.453
or understanding them, or
managing them, or using emotions.

00:25:21.810 --> 00:25:23.370
Perhaps thinking that you had

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:26.570
spectacular emotional intelligence

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:29.993
was a sign of not having
much of it at all.

00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:34.050
So that door closed.

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:36.730
We asked ourselves another question.

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:39.380
If we wanted to know if someone possessed

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:42.570
the skills of a great baseball player:

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:45.120
hitting, throwing, catching the ball,

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:47.600
running bases effectively.

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:50.763
How confident would we be of self-report?

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:54.490
The answer is not very.

00:25:54.490 --> 00:25:57.723
All ball players think they're
going to be the next A-Rod.

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:00.500
When I was a child, I thought I'd be

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:04.300
the next Carl Yastrzemski
on the Boston Red Sox,

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:06.483
while my brother played in the backyard.

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:10.460
But in fact, I barely got out of

00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:12.533
Little League with my pride intact.

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:16.380
Why would emotional intelligence

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:18.180
be any different than baseball?

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:21.460
If we wanted to know
whether someone had high EI,

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:24.773
we needed to assess these
skills as abilities,

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:27.360
and what would an ability measure

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:29.883
of emotional intelligence even look like?

00:26:30.730 --> 00:26:33.850
So asking ourselves these questions

00:26:33.850 --> 00:26:36.710
led to an answer that made more sense.

00:26:36.710 --> 00:26:39.780
And our ability-based measure
of emotional intelligence

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:43.350
has now been used in hundreds of studies.

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:45.650
Knowing that we didn't
have all the answers,

00:26:45.650 --> 00:26:49.860
and taking an inquisitive,
curious attitude allowed us

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:53.603
the opportunity to create
something relatively new.

00:26:56.860 --> 00:27:00.310
So what questions will you ask?

00:27:00.310 --> 00:27:02.823
What will spark your curiosity?

00:27:04.930 --> 00:27:07.330
Not long ago, I received an email from

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:09.993
a very proud Yale College parent.

00:27:11.403 --> 00:27:16.403
He told me about his son, who
heard 77 different speakers

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:21.103
during his first year at Yale, 77!

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:24.220
He had learned from thinkers and leaders

00:27:24.220 --> 00:27:27.150
across the political spectrum.

00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:28.790
He had attended events organized

00:27:28.790 --> 00:27:31.483
by a wide range of campus organizations.

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:34.603
What a way to spend a first year.

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:37.670
Could you do this and not

00:27:37.670 --> 00:27:40.423
change your mind about
something important?

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:43.590
And it turns out this student

00:27:43.590 --> 00:27:46.193
is also very good at asking questions.

00:27:47.640 --> 00:27:50.110
He's doing a project
where in the past year

00:27:50.110 --> 00:27:52.660
he has interviewed dozens of people,

00:27:52.660 --> 00:27:57.070
scholars, and activists, and
journalists, and entrepreneurs,

00:27:57.070 --> 00:27:58.833
from many different sectors.

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:03.500
Like so many students, faculty, and staff,

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:08.253
he is nurturing a culture
of curiosity at Yale.

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:12.760
Indeed the Yalies who have come before you

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:16.073
have asked a dazzling array of questions.

00:28:17.861 --> 00:28:19.833
I think of the pioneers of coeducation.

00:28:21.983 --> 00:28:24.310
Fifty years ago in 1969,

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:29.310
588 women came to study in Yale College.

00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:34.113
They entered what had long
been an all-male institution.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:38.593
And they asked questions that
hadn't been asked before.

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:42.430
We will commemorate this milestone

00:28:42.430 --> 00:28:46.120
along with the 150 anniversary of women

00:28:46.120 --> 00:28:50.823
entering Yale's School of
Art, throughout this year.

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:57.400
I think of Margaret Warner, Class of 1971,

00:28:57.400 --> 00:28:59.320
an award-winning journalist,

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:01.530
she knows how to ask brilliant questions

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:05.240
and has reported from
war zones for decades,

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:07.500
witnessing history first-hand,

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:10.723
trying to understand our world.

00:29:11.630 --> 00:29:16.200
I think too of Alice
Young, also Class of 1971.

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:19.540
She looked around this campus and asked,

00:29:19.540 --> 00:29:22.343
why weren't there more
students from public schools?

00:29:23.360 --> 00:29:25.340
So she became an ambassador for Yale

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:28.180
back in her home stage of Hawaii,

00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:30.310
an she was one of the founders

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:33.163
of the Asian American Students Alliance,

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:38.113
which also celebrates its
50th anniversary this year.

00:29:39.610 --> 00:29:42.100
And we remember other
important anniversaries

00:29:42.100 --> 00:29:46.210
and the curious students who
are part of these changes.

00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:49.050
In 1969, thanks to student efforts,

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:51.250
the Afro-American Cultural Center,

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:54.790
known as The House,
opened, and what is now

00:29:54.790 --> 00:29:59.143
the Department of African
American Studies was created.

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:02.600
That same year, students established

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:04.703
the Yale chapter of MEChA.

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:08.173
And I believe we owe a debt of gratitude,

00:30:09.366 --> 00:30:13.530
a debt of gratitude to all
the courageous pioneers,

00:30:13.530 --> 00:30:15.530
throughout our history,

00:30:15.530 --> 00:30:18.613
who have made Yale what it is today.

00:30:21.170 --> 00:30:24.490
What questions will you ask?

00:30:24.490 --> 00:30:26.590
How will your questions

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:30.723
transform Yale and improve the world?

00:30:32.150 --> 00:30:36.800
Your time at Yale is an
unparalleled opportunity

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:38.780
to engage with a wide range

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:42.093
of people, ideas, new experiences.

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:46.910
More than at any other point in your life,

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:49.960
you will have the means and opportunity

00:30:49.960 --> 00:30:52.300
to hear from and converse with

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:55.510
world-renowned experts in many fields.

00:30:55.510 --> 00:30:58.270
You will have the chance
to create knowledge,

00:30:58.270 --> 00:31:02.080
through rigorous research,
and attend arts, literary,

00:31:02.080 --> 00:31:06.313
and athletic events that
challenge and inspire you.

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:10.900
You will spend time with peers whose lives

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:14.333
have been widely different from your own.

00:31:16.550 --> 00:31:19.670
What if you nurtured your own curiosity

00:31:19.670 --> 00:31:24.513
by pushing yourself beyond the
familiar and the comfortable?

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:27.903
What would that look like for you?

00:31:29.320 --> 00:31:30.940
It might mean attending a talk

00:31:30.940 --> 00:31:33.980
on a topic you don't know much about,

00:31:33.980 --> 00:31:37.803
or by someone who doesn't
share your beliefs.

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:41.330
Or conducting research
in a Yale laboratory

00:31:41.330 --> 00:31:43.680
or collaborating on an exhibit

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:45.763
at one of our amazing museums.

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:48.770
Or perhaps your curiosity will be sparked

00:31:48.770 --> 00:31:51.220
by having coffee with a classmate

00:31:51.220 --> 00:31:54.500
who comes from a different
part of the world,

00:31:54.500 --> 00:31:57.633
or a different place on
the political spectrum.

00:31:59.230 --> 00:32:02.960
And when you do these things,
when you take advantage

00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:06.270
of the opportunities Yale makes possible,

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:08.683
what questions will you ask?

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:12.663
There is so much we don't know.

00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:18.310
Let us embrace together our humility,

00:32:18.310 --> 00:32:22.533
our willingness to admit
what we have yet to discover.

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:28.003
After all, if you knew all the answers,

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:30.200
you would not need Yale.

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:34.390
And if humanity knew all the answers,

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:36.903
the world would not need Yale.

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:41.050
So what questions will you ask

00:32:41.050 --> 00:32:45.713
today, tomorrow, the next day?

00:32:47.220 --> 00:32:50.780
And in the days, months, and years,

00:32:50.780 --> 00:32:54.333
after I have shaken your
hand at Commencement.

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:59.280
Let me know, let me know what questions

00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:04.280
you've asked that have changed your life.

00:33:05.810 --> 00:33:10.163
Good luck, Class of 2023.

00:33:10.163 --> 00:33:13.330
(audience applauding)

00:33:45.423 --> 00:33:48.840
(Yale Glee Club singing)

00:35:10.242 --> 00:35:13.659
(Yale Glee Club singing)

00:36:47.442 --> 00:36:50.609
(audience applauding)

00:37:08.500 --> 00:37:10.563
- My beloved Yale family,

00:37:11.460 --> 00:37:13.080
as we ready ourselves

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:16.090
to step out onto new ground,

00:37:16.090 --> 00:37:20.730
in this daunting yet
inspiring liminal space

00:37:20.730 --> 00:37:23.100
of the unknown next,

00:37:23.100 --> 00:37:28.060
we begin a poignant turn
of goodbyes and hellos

00:37:28.060 --> 00:37:32.140
that invite laughter, tears, challenge,

00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:36.610
choice, discovery, and pride.

00:37:36.610 --> 00:37:41.610
Let us go forth with deep
trust in certain knowledge,

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:46.690
that in this place each and
every one of you are welcome,

00:37:46.690 --> 00:37:51.690
that in this place, we
will both stumble and soar,

00:37:51.990 --> 00:37:55.520
that in this place love surrounds us,

00:37:55.520 --> 00:37:58.140
beauty can surprise us.

00:37:58.140 --> 00:38:01.963
In this place light and truth awaits us.

00:38:03.080 --> 00:38:05.350
On this day and every day forward,

00:38:05.350 --> 00:38:08.280
may all that is holy shine upon us,

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:12.390
and tenderly bless our every breath.

00:38:12.390 --> 00:38:16.840
May we go in Shalom, in Salam,

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:20.980
in Shanti, may we go in peace.

00:38:20.980 --> 00:38:22.063
Let us begin.

00:38:24.724 --> 00:38:27.391
(organ playing)

00:38:56.216 --> 00:38:58.633
(bell music)

